Here's a complete implementation for a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API using stdio transport:

---

## **Program.cs** — Stdio transport with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is reserved for JSON-RPC protocol
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## **WeatherTools.cs** — Tool class wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Units: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&units={units}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current temperature for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetTemperature(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var result = await client.GetFromJsonAsync<TempResult>(
            $"/temp?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return $"{result?.Temp}°C in {city}";
    }

    private record TempResult(double Temp, string Description);
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK uses two injection styles:

| Style | How it works |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Services passed via primary constructor. Class is instantiated per-call by the DI container. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services appear as method params — resolved automatically, hidden from the tool schema. |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<T>`, and any DI-registered service are **never** exposed in the JSON schema sent to the LLM
- Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the schema
- Register services in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.) — the MCP SDK resolves them at invocation time

**To scaffold this project:**
```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will start and await JSON-RPC messages on stdin. Connect it from VS Code, GitHub Copilot, or MCP Inspector for testing.
